No `.csproj` found, so this is a standalone file. Here's the lightest approach:

## Use `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+)

`TimeProvider` is built into the BCL — no DI container, no NuGet packages, just a constructor parameter:

**Production code** accepts `TimeProvider` (defaulting to `TimeProvider.System`):

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Test code** uses `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a small test-only NuGet):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

// You can also advance time:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
```

**Existing callers are unaffected** — the optional parameter means `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works and uses real time.

